# XCU 2.7-redirection
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.001
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.1
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7
class      required
says       The < operator opens the file named by its word for reading on
           standard input, or on the descriptor number written just before
           it.
test       t/001-input.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.002
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.2
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7
class      required
says       The > operator opens the named file for writing on standard
           output (or the descriptor written before it), creating it when
           absent and emptying it when present, and the redirection stays in
           effect for a descriptor opened with exec.
test       t/002-output.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.003
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7
class      required
says       Redirections can name any descriptor from 0 to 9: a file opened
           on each of 3 to 9 and written through that number receives the
           data.
test       t/003-descriptors-0-to-9.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.004
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.2
class      required
says       With set -C a > redirection does not replace an existing regular
           file (the command is not given the file and the file is
           unchanged), while >| does, a missing file is created, and a file
           that is not a regular file such as the null device is opened.
test       t/004-noclobber.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.005
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.3
class      required
says       The >> operator opens the named file for appending, so each write
           goes to the end, and creates it when absent.
test       t/005-append.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.006
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.7
class      required
says       The <> operator opens the named file for reading and writing on
           standard input (or the descriptor written before it) and creates
           it when absent.
test       t/006-read-write.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.007
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.5
class      required
says       The <& operator with a descriptor number makes the left
           descriptor (standard input by default) a copy of that open
           descriptor, and with - closes it; closing a descriptor that is
           not open is not an error.
test       t/007-dup-input.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.008
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.6
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7
class      required
says       The >& operator with a descriptor number makes the left
           descriptor (standard output by default) a copy of that open
           descriptor, with - closes it (closing a descriptor that is not
           open is no error), and redirections are processed from left to
           right so the order of 2>&1 and a file redirection matters.
test       t/008-dup-output.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.009
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7
class      required
says       A descriptor number must stand directly before the operator and
           be unquoted; a quoted number, a quoted operator, or a number set
           apart by a blank is not part of the redirection, and the
           redirection operator and its word do not reach the command as
           arguments.
test       t/009-number-and-quoting.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.010
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7
class      required
says       The word after > and similar operators undergoes tilde expansion,
           parameter expansion, command substitution, arithmetic expansion
           and quote removal.
test       t/010-target-expansions.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.011
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7
class      required
says       A non-interactive shell does not do filename expansion on the
           word of a redirection, so a pattern is used as the file name as
           written.
test       t/011-target-no-pathname.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.012
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7
class      required
says       When a command has several redirections they are carried out from
           first to last: with two output redirections both files are
           created and the last one is used; with two input redirections the
           last one is read.
test       t/012-order-of-evaluation.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.013
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7
class      disputed
says       When a redirection fails (a file that cannot be opened, a
           descriptor that is not open) the command is not run and the
           status is nonzero, and the shell goes on with the next command.
note       disputed (Q-170): the text says a failure to open a file, or a
           copy of a descriptor that is not open, makes the redirection
           fail, and the error table says the shell goes on after such an
           error in a utility, but it never says in words that the command
           is then not run, nor what status it gets (only the exec page and
           the no-command-name case give 1 to 125 or more than zero).
           Refusal to run and a nonzero status are read here as meant.
test       t/013-failed-redirection.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.014
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.4
class      required
says       A here-document ends at the first line that holds only the
           delimiter: a line with the delimiter and a trailing blank, or
           with a leading blank, is part of the text.
test       t/014-here-delimiter-line.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.015
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.4
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.3
class      required
says       When no part of the delimiter word is quoted, the lines of a
           here-document undergo parameter expansion, command substitution
           and arithmetic expansion, a backslash acts as in double quotes
           (so it escapes a dollar sign, backquote, backslash or newline and
           is otherwise literal), and quotation marks are ordinary
           characters.
test       t/015-here-unquoted.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.016
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.4
class      required
says       If any part of the delimiter word is quoted, the delimiter is the
           word with quote removal and the lines of the here-document are
           used as they are, with no expansion and backslash-newline kept.
test       t/016-here-quoted.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.017
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.4
class      required
says       With <<- leading tab characters are removed from each line of the
           text and from the delimiter line (spaces are not), and tabs that
           come from expansions are kept.
test       t/017-here-strip-tabs.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.018
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.4
class      required
says       With two here-document operators on one line the text of the
           first is read first and belongs to the first, the next lines to
           the second.
test       t/018-here-two-on-a-line.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.019
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.4
class      required
says       A here-document of a command that is not executed is still read
           from the input, without any expansion being performed.
test       t/019-here-not-executed.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.020
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.4
class      required
says       A here-document can be attached to a descriptor number written
           before the operator.
test       t/020-here-descriptor.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.021
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.4
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.3
class      required
says       A here-document inside a $( ) takes its text from the lines
           inside the substitution, up to its delimiter line.
test       t/021-here-in-cmdsub.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.022
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.4
class      required
says       For finding the delimiter line, the end of a command string given
           to sh -c counts as a newline.
test       t/022-here-command-string.sh

assertion  XCU.2.7-redirection.023
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.7.4
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.2.1
class      required
says       With an unquoted delimiter, backslash-newline is removed while
           the delimiter line is looked for, so a delimiter that line
           continuation has glued to the line before it is not recognized.
test       t/023-here-continuation.sh
